[Bertie] All this triumph had produced a sort of grim geniality in her, and she was almost motherly when we met.
[Aunt Agatha] I am so glad you were able to come, Bertie.
[Aunt Agatha] The air will do you so much good.
[Aunt Agatha] Far better for you than spending your time in stuffy London night clubs.
[Bertie] Oh, ah.
[Aunt Agatha] You will meet some pleasant people, too.
[Aunt Agatha] I want to introduce you to a Miss Hemmingway and her brother, who have become great friends of mine.
[Aunt Agatha] Aline Hemmingway is just the girl I should like to see you marry, Bertie.
[Aunt Agatha] You ought to be thinking of getting married.
[Aunt Agatha] Marriage might make something of you.
[Bertie] Here, I say!
[Aunt Agatha] Bertie!
[Bertie] Yes, but I say...
[Aunt Agatha] It is young men like you, Bertie, who make the person with the future of the race at heart despair.
[Aunt Agatha] You do nothing but waste your time on frivolous pleasures.
[Aunt Agatha] You are simply an antisocial animal, a drone.
[Aunt Agatha] Bertie, it is imperative that you marry.
[Bertie] But, dash it all...
[Aunt Agatha] Yes! You should be breeding children to...
[Bertie] No, really, I say, please!